    "lawCode": "Add §169, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9370\n\nSPONSOR: JACKSON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to presump-\ntive evidence for individuals who participated in the World Trade Center\nrescue, recovery and clean-up operations\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation provides a presumption for workers' compensation claims\nfor any health impairment or death of individuals who participated in\nthe World Trade Center rescue, recovery and clean-up operations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new Section 169 to the Workers' Compensation Law to\nprovide that Centers for Disease Control and Prevention World Trade\nCenter Health Program Certification shall be deemed to be presumptive\nmedical evidence of a causally related diagnosis for an individual\ncovered under such section, and provides for a two-year period after the\neffective date of the act during which claims that were denied by the\nworkers' compensation board may be reified.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere is no doubt that World Trade Center rescue and recovery workers'\nexposure to toxins during clean-up operations has been extensive and\nseriously damaging to their health. In fact, the CDC's World Trade\nCenter Health Program has defined a list of certified illnesses caused\nby this exposure. The program provides funding to first responders for\nmedical treatment and complications derived from their response to the\n9/11 terrorist attacks. Currently, the workers' compensation law does\nnot compel the NYS Workers' Compensation Board to accept the certif-\nications of the CDC's World Trade Center Health Program as presumptive\ncausation of certified illnesses. This has resulted in many valid claims\nbeing denied.\n\nMany of the first responders & rescue and recovery workers as defined\nunder article 8-A who responded to the 9/11 terrorist attacks, have had\ntheir illnesses certified by the CDC \"World Trade Center Health\nProgram.\" These workers or their surviving dependents have filed a time-\nly claim for benefits with the NYS Workers' Compensation Board and have\nbeen denied without cause. Other dependents of workers who died of 9/11\ncertified illnesses are barred from filing claims for workers' compen-\nsation benefits because the statute of limitations expired before the\nCDC \"World Trade Center Health Program\" certified illnesses was accepted\nas presumptive cause.\n\nIf enacted, this legislation would help first responders and rescue and\nrecovery workers who contracted an illness or died of a certified\nillness get the benefits that they and their families deserve.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n \nSTATE AND LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
